BREAKFAST TALLY

Breakfast Tally — a real-world task using only today's concepts: simple counting with dictionaries, get() or the in check to grow counts, looping over a dictionary, items(), comparisons, if / else, while validation, variables, input(), and printing. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A breakfast vendor at the motor park serves six things: rice, beans, yam, bread, tea, eggs. Customers shout their orders one after another, and her apprentice writes tallies in a book - each item and how many people ordered it. Today that book is a dictionary.

The program must:
- Ask "How many orders: " and keep asking until it is at least 1.
- Read the orders one by one, each typed as one word. The FIRST time an item appears it starts counting from 1 - build the tally dictionary as the orders come in.
- An order that is not on the menu (like pizza) prints "Not on the menu" and does NOT enter the tally.
- After the last order, print the tally, one line per item in the order the items first appeared: RICE: 3 orders
- Then the winner: TOP SELLER: rice (3 orders) - if two items tie, the one that reached that count FIRST wins.

Sample run:

How many orders: 5
Order 1: rice
Order 2: tea
Order 3: rice
Order 4: pizza
Not on the menu
Order 5: rice

RICE: 3 orders
TEA: 1 order
TOP SELLER: rice (3 orders)

Edge cases to handle: every order the same item (one line in the tally), an order of 1 printing "1 order" not "1 orders", an unknown item mixed in the middle (skipped, counting continues), a tie where the second item reached the count first (it wins), and checking that pizza never appears in the tally.
